# BayLang Documentation

File `baylang.rule` contains common BayLang syntax and how it use.

BayLang is a full-stack programming language that compiles into PHP and JavaScript. It uses a single codebase for both the server and the client side.

**BayLang Features:**
- Cross-platform application development (websites, CRM and ERP systems, mobile and desktop applications)
- Reactivity: automatic page update when data model changes
- MVC architecture implementation
- Server-side rendering (SSR) support
- Website builder support
- MySQL and SQLite support, with the possibility of adding other DBMS

# Basic Syntax

## Types

```
int count = 0;
double price = 9.99;
bool is_active = false;
string name = "Hello";
Vector items = [];
Map data = {};
```

## Variables

Use `snake_case` for variables and `CamelCase` for methods.

## Conditions

```
if (a > b)
{
	print("A is greater than B");
}
else
{
	print("A is less than or equal to B");
}
```

## Loops

```
for (int i = 0; i < items.count(); i++)
{
	print(items.get(i));
}
```


# Working with Strings

Strings are immutable. Concatenate with `~`.

```
string message = "Hello";
string name = "User";
string greeting = message ~ " " ~ name;
```

**String library (`rs`):**

```
int rs::strlen(string s);
string rs::substr(string s, int start, int len);
string rs::lower(string s);
string rs::upper(string s);
int rs::compare(string a, string b);
int rs::replace(string search, string item, string s);
string rs::str_repeat(string s, int n);
string rs::split(string delimiter, string s, int limit = -1);
string rs::join(string ch, Vector<string> arr);
string rs::join_path(Vector arr);
string rs::trim(string s, string ch = "");
int rs::indexOf(string s, string search, int offset = 0);
string rs::format(string s, Dict params = null);
```

**Example:**

```
string message = rs::format("Hello %username%", {"username": "User"});
```


# Data Structures

## Vector (Array)

A Vector is an ordered list of elements. You can add, remove, and search items.

```
Vector arr = [];
```

**Basic operations:**

```
/* Count elements */
print(arr.count());

/* Get element by index */
print(arr.get(index));
print(arr.get(index, default_value));

/* Set element */
arr.set(index, value);

/* First and last */
arr.first();
arr.last();
```

**Adding and removing:**

```
arr.push(item);
arr.remove(index);
arr.insert(index, new_item);

Vector new_arr = arr.concat(["new item"]);
arr.appendItems(items);

/* Get a portion of the array */
arr.slice(offset);
arr.slice(offset, length);
```

**Searching:**

```
int index = arr.indexOf(value);

string item = arr.find(bool (string item) => rs::strlen(item) != 0);
int index = arr.findIndex(bool (string item) => rs::strlen(item) != 0);
```

**Functional programming:**

```
Vector new_arr = arr.map(int (int item) => item * 2);

int sum = arr.reduce(
	int (int sum, int value) => sum + value, 0
);

Vector new_arr = arr.filter(bool (int item) => item != 0);

int sum = 0;
arr.each(void (int item, int index) use (sum) {
	sum += item;
});
```

**Convert Vector to Map:**

```
Map items = arr.transition(
	Vector (int value, int index)
	{
		return [index, value];
	}
);
```

**Sorting:**

```
arr.sort();
arr.sort(int (int a, int b) => a > b);
```

**Manipulation:**

```
arr.reverse();
arr.removeDuplicates();
arr.flatten();
```


## Map (Dictionary)

A Map stores key-value pairs. Keys are strings.

```
Map items = {};

items.set(key, value);
items.get(key);

items.concat({"key": "value"});
```

**Functional programming:**

```
Map new_map = items.map(
	int (int value, string key) => value * 2
);

Map new_map = items.mapWithKeys(
	Vector (int value, string key) => [key, value * 2]
);

int sum = items.reduce(
	int (int sum, int value, string key) => sum + value, 0
);

Map new_map = items.filter(
	bool (int value, string key) => value != 0
);

int sum = 0;
items.each(
	void (int value, string key) use (sum) {
		sum += item;
	}
);

Vector arr = items.transition(
	int (int value, string key) => value
);
```


# Components

> **⚠️ Important:** Every component MUST be wrapped in a `<class>` tag. This is the root element — without it the component will not compile.

**Required structure:**

```
<class name="App.Components.IndexPage.IndexPage">

<style>
	/* CSS styles here */
</style>

<template>
	<!-- HTML template here -->
</template>

<script>
	/* BayLang logic here */
</script>

</class>
```

> **⚠️ Common mistake:** Do not forget to open `<class>` at the top and close `</class>` at the bottom. All other blocks (`<style>`, `<template>`, `<script>`) must be inside `<class>`.

## Connecting Modules with `<use>`

To use external modules or other components, place `<use>` tags **before** the `<script>` block (but still inside `<class>`):

```
<class name="App.Components.IndexPage.IndexPage">

<use name="Runtime.Widget.Button" component="true" />

<style>
	.index_page {
		padding: 5px;
	}
</style>

<template>
	<div class="index_page">
		<Button>Click</Button>
	</div>
</template>

<script>
	string message = "Hello world!";
</script>

</class>
```

**Order inside `<class>`:**
1. `<use>` — module/component imports (before `<script>`)
2. `<style>` — CSS styles
3. `<template>` — HTML markup
4. `<script>` — component logic

## Props

Props are values passed from a parent component.

```
<script>
props string name = "";
</script>
```

## Computed Values

Computed values update automatically when their dependencies change.

```
<script>
computed string user_name()
{
	return this.name;
}
</script>
```

## Events

```
<template>
	<button @event:click="this.onClick()">
		Click
	</button>
</template>

<script>
string message = "User";

void onClick()
{
	this.message ~= "!";
}
</script>
```

## Using Other Components

```
<class name="App.Example">

<use name="Runtime.Widget.Button" component="true" />

<template>
	<Button>Click</Button>
</template>

</class>
```

## Slots

Slots allow you to pass template content into a component.

**Slot definition:**

```
<template>
	<div class="box">
		<div class="box_header">
			%render this.renderSlot("title");
		</div>
		<div class="box_content">
			%render this.renderSlot("content");
		</div>
	</div>
</template>
```

**Using the component with slots:**

```
<Box>
	<slot name="title">Title</slot>
	<slot name="content"><p>Content</p></slot>
</Box>
```


# Models

Models hold the application state and business logic. They are part of the frontend and control how data is stored on the client.

```
namespace App;

use Runtime.BaseModel;
use Runtime.RenderContainer;
use Runtime.Serializer.ObjectType;
use Runtime.Serializer.StringType;

class PageModel extends BaseModel
{
	string component = classof ExamplePage;
	string name = "";

	void initParams(Map params)
	{
		parent(params);
	}

	void initWidget(Map params)
	{
		parent(params);
	}

	static void serialize(ObjectType rules)
	{
		parent(rules);
		rules.addType("name", new StringType());
	}

	async void loadData(RenderContainer container)
	{
		await parent(container);
	}

	void buildTitle(RenderContainer container)
	{
		this.layout.setPageTitle("Page");
	}
}
```

**Key lifecycle methods:**

- `initParams` — set up model parameters
- `initWidget` — create and configure child models
- `serialize` — define which data gets sent to the frontend
- `loadData` — fetch data from the backend API
- `buildTitle` — set the page title

## Example Model: User

The `User` model is a common example of a data model in BayLang. It typically stores user information such as name, email, and ID.

```
namespace App.Models;

use Runtime.BaseDTO;
use Runtime.Serializer.IntegerType;
use Runtime.Serializer.ObjectType;
use Runtime.Serializer.StringType;

class User extends BaseDTO
{
	int id = 0;
	string name = "";
	string email = "";

	static void serialize(ObjectType rules)
	{
		parent(rules);
		rules.addType("id", new IntegerType());
		rules.addType("name", new StringType());
		rules.addType("email", new StringType());
	}
}
```

**Key properties:**

- `id` — unique identifier for the user
- `name` — user's display name
- `email` — user's email address

The `serialize` function defines which fields are sent to the frontend. This ensures that only necessary data is transmitted, improving security and performance.

## Loading Models in PageModel

Models can be loaded in `PageModel` via API calls or manually. This is essential for populating the page with data from the backend.

### Example: Loading a User via API

This example shows how to load a User model from the backend using an API call inside `loadData`.

```
namespace App;

use Runtime.ApiResult;
use Runtime.BaseModel;
use Runtime.RenderContainer;
use Runtime.Serializer.IntegerType;
use Runtime.Serializer.ObjectType;
use Runtime.Serializer.StringType;
use Runtime.Web.RouteInfo;
use App.Components.UserPage.UserPage;
use App.Models.User;

class UserPageModel extends BaseModel
{
	string component = classof UserPage;
	int user_id = 0;
	User user = null;
	
	void initParams(Map params)
	{
		parent(params);
		
		if (params.has("user_id"))
		{
			this.user_id = params.get("user_id");
		}
	}

	void initWidget(Map params)
	{
		parent(params);
	}

	static void serialize(ObjectType rules)
	{
		parent(rules);
		rules.addType("user_id", new IntegerType());
		rules.addType("user", new ObjectType{
			"class_name": classof User,
		});
	}

	async void loadData(RenderContainer container)
	{
		await parent(container);
		
		RouteInfo route = this.layout.get("route");
		this.user_id = route.matches.get("user_id");
		await this.loadUser(this.user_id);
	}

	async void loadUser(int id)
	{
		ApiResult result = this.layout.sendApi({
			"api_name": "app.user",
			"method_name": "item",
			"data":
			{
				"pk":
				{
					"id": id
				}
			}
		});
		
		if (result.isSuccess())
		{
			this.user = static::filter("user", result.data.get("item"));
		}
	}
}
```

# Layout

`this.layout` is the global application model. It gives access to:

- Page title (`this.layout.setPageTitle(...)`)
- Current route
- List of components and models


