# BayLang Template Syntax

## Loops in Templates

Instead of using Vue-like directives such as `<div v-for="item in items">`, use the `%for` statement in BayLang templates.

**Example:**

```
%for (Map item in items)
{
	<div class="item">
		{{ item.name }}
	</div>
}
```

## Conditional Rendering

Instead of using Vue-like directives such as `v-if`, use the `%if` statement in BayLang templates.

**Example:**

```
%if (this.model.is_active)
{
	<div class="active">
		This content is active.
	</div>
}
```

## Combining Loops and Conditionals

You can combine `%for` and `%if` in templates for more complex logic.

**Example:**

```
%for (Map item in items)
{
	%if (item.is_visible)
	{
		<div class="item visible">
			{{ item.name }}
		</div>
	}
	else
	{
		<div class="item hidden">
			{{ item.name }} (hidden)
		</div>
	}
}
```

## Data Binding

BayLang supports data binding in templates using `{{ }}` syntax. You can bind component attributes to dynamic values or use static strings.

**Example with data binding:**

```
<Input name={{ item.name }} value={{ item.value }} />
```

**Example without data binding (static values):**

```
<Input name="email" value={{ item.email }} />
```

**Combined example with loops:**

```
%for (Map item in items)
{
	<div class="input_group">
		<Input name={{ item.name }} value={{ item.value }} />
	</div>
}
```

**Example with conditional binding:**

```
%if (item.is_required)
{
	<Input name={{ item.name }} value={{ item.value }} required="true" />
}
else
{
	<Input name={{ item.name }} value={{ item.value }} />
}
```

**Explanation:**

- `{{ item.name }}` — dynamic binding to the `name` attribute
- `"email"` — static string value (no binding)
- `{{ item.value }}` — dynamic binding to the `value` attribute

## Event Handling

BayLang uses a special syntax for handling DOM events in templates. You can bind event handlers directly to elements using the `@event:` prefix.

**Example: Input with value change event**

```
<Input value={{ item.value }} event:valueChange="this.onChange(event.value)" />
```

**Example: Button with click event**

```
<Button class="button button--primary" @event:click="this.onClick()">Click</Button>
```

**Explanation:**

- `event:valueChange` — attaches an event handler to the `valueChange` event of the input
- `this.onChange(event.value)` — the component method `onChange` with the new value from the event
- `@event:click` — shorthand for handling the click event
- `this.onClick()` — the component method `onClick` when the button is clicked

**Full example with events and data binding:**

```
%for (Map item in items)
{
	<div class="input_group">
		<Input value={{ item.value }} event:valueChange="this.onChange(event.value, item)" />
		<Button class="button button--primary" @event:click="this.onClick(item)">Select</Button>
	</div>
}
```

# ORM (Object-Relational Mapping)

BayLang provides a powerful ORM system for working with databases. The ORM architecture consists of:
- **Connection** — Database connection management
- **Query** — SQL query building
- **Record** — Data models and entities
- **Relation** — Query execution and data retrieval

## Defining Records (Data Models)

Records represent database tables as objects:

```
namespace App.Database;

use Runtime.BaseObject;
use Runtime.ORM.Record;
use Runtime.ORM.Annotations.AutoIncrement;
use Runtime.ORM.Annotations.BigIntType;
use Runtime.ORM.Annotations.Primary;
use Runtime.ORM.Annotations.StringType;
use Runtime.ORM.Annotations.DateTimeType;

class Item extends Record
{
	/**
	 * Returns table name
	 */
	pure string getTableName() => "items";
	
	/**
	 * Returns table schema
	 */
	pure memorize Collection<BaseObject> schema() =>
	[
		/* Fields */
		new BigIntType{"name": "id"},
		new StringType{"name": "name"},
		new StringType{"name": "description"},
		new BigIntType{"name": "category_id"},
		new DateTimeType{"name": "gmtime_add", "autocreate": true},
		new DateTimeType{"name": "gmtime_edit", "autoupdate": true},
		
		/* Index */
		new AutoIncrement{"name": "id"},
		new Primary{"keys": ["id"]},
	];
}
```

## Query Building

Build SQL queries using the Query object:

```
use Runtime.ORM.Query;

Query q = new Query()
	.select(["id", "name"])
	.from("items")
	.where("id", "=", "1")
;
```

## Working with Relations

Relations provide methods to interact with the database:

```
use Runtime.ORM.Connection;
use Runtime.ORM.Query;
use Runtime.ORM.QueryFilter;
use Runtime.ORM.Record;
use Runtime.ORM.Relation;

Relation<Item> relation = new Relation(classof Item);
```

### Creating and Saving Records

```
/* Create a new item */
Record<Item> item = relation.createRecord({
	"name": "New Item",
	"description": "Item description"
});

/* Or create and save directly */
Item item = new Item();
item.name = "Item Name";
await item.save();

/* Update existing item */
item.name = "Updated Name";
await item.save();

/* Delete item */
await item.delete();
```

### Fetching Data

```
/* Get all items */
QueryResult<Map> rows = await relation.fetchAll(
	relation.select()
);

/* Get a single record by primary key */
Record<Item> item = await relation.findByPk({"id": 1});

/* Get a single record by ID */
Record<Item> item = await relation.findById(1);

/* Find records with filter */
Vector<QueryFilter> filter = [
	new QueryFilter("name", "%like%", "Item name")
];
Record<Item> item = await relation.findRecord(filter);

/* Find or create */
Record<Item> item = await relation.findOrCreate({
	"name": "Unique Item"
});
```

### Advanced Query Examples

```
/* Count items */
Query q = new Query()
	.select(["id", "name"])
	.from("items")
	.where("category_id", "=", "1")
	.limit(10)
	.page(0)
;

QueryResult<Map> rows = await relation.fetchAll(q);

/* Update with filter */
Query q = new Query()
	.update("items")
	.where("category_id", "=", "1")
	.values({
		"status": "active"
	})
;

await relation.execute(q);

/* Delete with filter */
Query q = new Query()
	.delete("items")
	.where("id", "=", "1")
;

await relation.execute(q);
```

### Working with Record Data

```
Record<Item> item = await relation.findByPk({"id": 1});

/* Get all data */
Map data = item.all();

/* Get old data (before changes) */
Map old_data = item.old();

/* Get specific fields */
Map partial_data = item.intersect(["id", "name"]);

/* Check if record has changed */
bool is_changed = item.isChanged();

/* Check if record is new (not saved yet) */
bool is_new = item.isNew();

/* Check if record was loaded from database */
bool is_update = item.isUpdate();

/* Assign new data */
item.assign({
	"name": "New Name",
	"description": "Updated description"
});
await item.save();
```

# API Interaction

Api interaction is handled via the `sendApi` method. This allows communication between frontend components and backend services.

## Basic API Call

**Example:**

```
ApiResult result = await this.layout.sendApi({
	"api_name": "app.catalog",
	"method_name": "search",
	"data":
	{
		"search": "product",
		"page": 0,
		"limit": 10,
	},
});

if (result.isSuccess())
{
	/* Handle successful response */
	Vector<Map> items = result.data.get("items");
}
else
{
	/* Handle error */
	rtl::print(result.getError());
}
```

## Creating API Endpoints

### Basic API Class

```
namespace App.Api;

use Runtime.Serializer.MapType;
use Runtime.Serializer.StringType;
use Runtime.Web.Annotations.ApiMethod;
use Runtime.Web.BaseApi;

class ExampleApi extends BaseApi
{
	pure string getApiName() => "app.example";
	
	
	/**
	 * Returns data rules
	 */
	void getDataRules(MapType rules)
	{
		rules.addType("name", new StringType());
	}
	
	
	@ApiMethod{ "name": "index" }
	async void actionIndex()
	{
		this.filterData();
		
		string name = this.data.get("name");
		this.success({
			"data": {
				"name": name,
			}
		})
	}
}
```

**Register API in module:**

```
namespace App;

use Runtime.Entity.Entity;
use Runtime.Web.Annotations.Api;

class ModuleDescription
{
	pure string getModuleName() => "App";
	pure string getModuleVersion() => "1.0";
	pure Map<string> requiredModules() => {
		"Runtime": "*"
	};
	pure Vector<Entity> modules() => [
		new Api("App.Api.ExampleApi")
	];
}
```

### SearchApi - For Reading Data

The `SearchApi` module provides functionality for searching and retrieving data from a database.

**Key Features:**
- Automatic query generation based on request parameters
- Input data validation
- Flexible field definition
- Query customization (filtering, sorting, joins)

**Example:**

```
namespace App.Admin.Api;

use Runtime.Serializer.MapType;
use Runtime.ORM.Query;
use Runtime.Web.Annotations.ApiMethod;
use Runtime.Widget.Api.SearchApi;
use Runtime.WordPress.Admin.AdminMiddleware;
use App.Database.Item;


class ItemSearchApi extends SearchApi
{
	pure string getApiName() => "admin.item";
	pure string getRecordName() => classof Item;
	
	Vector<Middleware> getMiddleware() =>
	[
		new AdminMiddleware(),
	];
	
	void getDataRules(MapType rules)
	{
	}
	
	Vector<string> getItemFields(string action)
	{
		Map fields =
		[
			"id",
			"name"
		];
		if (action == "convert")
		{
			/* Add inner join fields */
		}
		return fields;
	}
	
	async void buildQuery(Query q)
	{
		await parent(q);
	}
	
	@ApiMethod{ "name": "search" }
	async void actionSearch()
	{
		await parent();
	}
	
	@ApiMethod{ "name": "item" }
	async void actionItem()
	{
		await parent();
	}
}
```

### SaveApi - For Creating/Updating/Deleting Data

The `SaveApi` module provides functionality for saving (creating/updating) and deleting data in a database.

**Key Features:**
- Automatic save/update detection (based on primary key)
- Strict data validation
- Field management (allowed fields for saving)
- Lifecycle hooks for business logic

**Example:**

```
namespace App.Api;

use Runtime.Widget.Api.SaveApi;
use Runtime.Widget.Api.Rules.UniqueRules;
use Runtime.WordPress.Admin.AdminMiddleware;
use App.Database.Item;


class ItemSaveApi extends SaveApi
{
	pure string getApiName() => "app.item";
	pure string getRecordName() => classof Item;
	
	Vector<Middleware> getMiddleware() =>
	[
		new AdminMiddleware(),
	];
	
	Vector<BaseRule> rules() =>
	[
		new UniqueRules{"field_name": "name"},
	];
	
	void getDataRules(MapType rules)
	{
	}
	
	void getItemRules(MapType rules)
	{
		rules.addType("id", new IntegerType());
		rules.addType("name", new Required());
		rules.addType("name", new StringType());
		rules.addType("description", new StringType());
		rules.addType("category_id", new IntegerType());
	}
	
	Vector<string> getItemFields() =>
	[
		"id",
		"name",
		"description",
		"category_id",
		"gmtime_add",
		"gmtime_edit",
	];
	
	Vector<string> getSaveFields() =>
	[
		"name",
		"description",
		"category_id",
	];
	
	async void buildQuery(Query q)
	{
		await parent(q);
		
		if (this.foreign_key && this.foreign_key.has("category_id"))
		{
			q.where("category_id", "=", this.foreign_key.get("category_id"));
		}
	}
	
	async void onSaveBefore()
	{
		await parent();
	}
	
	async void onSaveAfter()
	{
		await parent();
	}
	
	async void onDeleteBefore()
	{
		await parent();
	}
	
	async void onDeleteAfter()
	{
	}
	
	@ApiMethod{ "name": "save" }
	async void actionSave()
	{
		await parent();
	}
	
	@ApiMethod{ "name": "delete" }
	async void actionDelete()
	{
		await parent();
	}
}
```

# Example: User Management Page

This section demonstrates a complete user management page with listing, adding, editing, and deleting functionality using BayLang components and models.

## UserPage Component

The `UserPage` component displays a table of users with action buttons for managing them.

```
<class name="App.UserPage">

<use name="Runtime.Widget.Button" component="true" />
<use name="Runtime.Widget.RowButtons" component="true" />
<use name="Runtime.Widget.Table.TableWrap" component="true" />

<style>
.user_page{
}
</style>

<template>
    <div class="user_page">
        <RowButtons>
            <Button class="button button--primary"
                @event:click="this.model.manager.showAddDialog()"
            >
                Add
            </Button>
        </RowButtons>
        <TableWrap model={{ this.model.manager }}>
            <slot name="row_buttons" args="Map item, Map field, int row_number">
                <RowButtons>
                    <Button class="button"
                        @event:click="this.model.manager.showEditDialog(item)"
                    >
                        Edit
                    </Button>
                    <Button class="button button--danger"
                        @event:click="this.model.manager.showDeleteDialog(item)"
                    >
                        Delete
                    </Button>
                </RowButtons>
            </slot>
        </TableWrap>
    </div>
</template>

</class>
```

**Key features:**
- Uses `TableWrap` component to display data
- Integrates `RowButtons` for action controls
- `showAddDialog()`, `showEditDialog()`, `showDeleteDialog()` methods for CRUD operations

## UserPageModel Model

The `UserPageModel` manages the state and logic for the user page, including table configuration and CRUD operations.

```
namespace App;

use Runtime.ApiResult;
use Runtime.BaseModel;
use Runtime.RenderContainer;
use Runtime.Serializer.IntegerType;
use Runtime.Serializer.ObjectType;
use Runtime.Serializer.StringType;
use Runtime.Web.RouteInfo;
use Runtime.Widget.Table.TableManager;

class UserPageModel extends BaseModel
{
	string component = classof UserPage;
	TableManager manager = null;
	
	
	/**
	 * Serialize object
	 */
	static void serialize(ObjectType rules)
	{
		parent(rules);
		rules.addType("manager", new ObjectType());
	}
	
	
	/**
	 * Init params
	 */
	void initParams(Map params)
	{
		parent(params);
	}
	
	
	/**
	 * Init widget
	 */
	void initWidget(Map params)
	{
		parent(params);
		
		/* Инициализация TableManager */
		this.manager = this.createWidget(classof TableManager, {
			"autoload": true,
			"api_name": "app.user",
			"title": method this.getTitle,
			"item_rules": {
				"name": new StringType(),
				"email": new StringType(),
				"role": new StringType(),
			},
			"primary_rules": {
				"id": new IntegerType(),
			},
			"form_fields":
			[
				{
					"name": "name",
					"label": "Name",
					"component": "Runtime.Widget.Input",
				},
				{
					"name": "email",
					"label": "Email",
					"component": "Runtime.Widget.Input",
				},
				{
					"name": "role",
					"label": "Role",
					"component": "Runtime.Widget.Select",
					"props":
					{
						"options":
						[
							{"key": "admin", "value": "Admin"},
							{"key": "user", "value": "User"},
						]
					}
				}
			],
			"table_fields":
			[
				{
					"name": "row_number",
				},
				{
					"name": "name",
					"label": "Name",
				},
				{
					"name": "email",
					"label": "Email",
				},
				{
					"name": "role",
					"label": "Role",
				},
				{
					"name": "buttons",
					"slot": "row_buttons"
				}
			]
		});
	}
	
	
	/**
	 * Load data
	 */
	async void loadData(RenderContainer container)
	{
		await parent(container);
	}
	
	
	/**
	 * Build title
	 */
	void buildTitle(RenderContainer container)
	{
		this.layout.setPageTitle("Users page");
	}
	
	
	/**
	 * Returns title
	 */
	string getTitle(string action, Map title)
	{
		if (action == "add") return "Create user";
		else if (action == "edit") return "Edit user";
		else if (action == "delete") return "Delete user";
		else if (action == "delete_message") return "Delete";
		return "";
	}
}
```

**Key features:**
- Uses `TableManager` for data grid functionality
- Configures form fields for add/edit dialogs
- Defines table columns for display
- Implements title generation for different actions

# TableWrap and TableManager

`TableWrap` and `TableManager` are components for displaying and managing tabular data in BayLang applications.

## TableWrap Component

The `TableWrap` component provides a flexible container for displaying data with additional slots for customization.

### Slots

`TableWrap` supports several slots to customize its appearance and functionality:

- **`table`** — Main slot for table content. Allows replacing the default table with a custom layout (e.g., a list or grid view).
- **`top_buttons`** — Slot for buttons/controls at the top of the table (e.g., "Add", "Export").
- **`filter`** — Slot for filter controls (search, dropdowns, etc.).
- **`row_buttons`** — Slot for action buttons per row (e.g., "Edit", "Delete").

### Example: Custom Layout with TableWrap

Instead of a traditional table, you can use the `table` slot to display data as a list:

```
<class name="App.Components.CustomPage.CustomPage">

<use name="Runtime.Widget.Table.TableWrap" component="true" />

<template>
    <TableWrap model={{ this.model.manager }}>
        <!-- Custom top buttons -->
        <slot name="top_buttons">
            <Button class="button button--primary">Add New</Button>
        </slot>
        
        <!-- Custom filter -->
        <slot name="filter">
            <Input placeholder="Search..." />
        </slot>
        
        <!-- Custom table layout (list view) -->
        <slot name="table">
            %for (Map item in this.model.manager.items)
            {
                <div class="list-item">
                    <h3>{{ item.name }}</h3>
                    <p>{{ item.description }}</p>
                </div>
            }
        </slot>
        
        <!-- Row buttons -->
        <slot name="row_buttons" args="Map item, Map field, int row_number">
            <Button @event:click="this.model.manager.showEditDialog(item)">Edit</Button>
        </slot>
    </TableWrap>
</template>

</class>
```

## TableManager Configuration

`TableManager` is a model that manages table data, including configuration for form fields and table columns.

### Form Fields Configuration

Form fields define how data is displayed and edited in dialogs. Each field can have several properties:

- **`name`** — Field identifier.
- **`label`** — Display label.
- **`component`** — UI component (e.g., `Runtime.Widget.Input`, `Runtime.Widget.Select`).
- **`value`** — Function to extract the display value from an item.
- **`visible`** — Function to determine if the field should be displayed.

#### Using `value` Function

The `value` function allows you to customize how a field's value is extracted from the data item.

**Example:**

```
"form_fields":
[
    {
        "name": "full_name",
        "label": "Full Name",
        "component": "Runtime.Widget.Input",
        "value": string (Map item) => item.get("first_name") ~ " " ~ item.get("last_name")
    }
]
```

#### Using `visible` Function

The `visible` function controls whether a field is displayed based on conditions.

**Example:**

```
"form_fields":
[
    {
        "name": "admin_notes",
        "label": "Admin Notes",
        "component": "Runtime.Widget.Textarea",
        "visible": bool (Map item) => item.get("role") == "admin"
    }
]
```

### Example: TableManager with Custom Form Fields

```
namespace App;

use Runtime.BaseModel;
use Runtime.Serializer.IntegerType;
use Runtime.Serializer.ObjectType;
use Runtime.Serializer.StringType;
use Runtime.Widget.Table.TableManager;

class UserPageModel extends BaseModel
{
	TableManager manager = null;
	
	void initWidget(Map params)
	{
		parent(params);
		
		this.manager = this.createWidget(classof TableManager, {
			"api_name": "app.user",
			"form_fields":
			[
				{
					"name": "full_name",
					"label": "Full Name",
					"component": "Runtime.Widget.Input",
					"value": string (Map item) => item.get("first_name") ~ " " ~ item.get("last_name")
				},
				{
					"name": "admin_notes",
					"label": "Admin Notes",
					"component": "Runtime.Widget.Textarea",
					"visible": bool (Map item) => item.get("role") == "admin"
				}
			],
			"table_fields":
			[
				{
					"name": "full_name",
					"label": "Name"
				},
				{
					"name": "email",
					"label": "Email"
				}
			]
		});
	}
}
```

# TableManager with SelectModel (Dynamic Options)

This section demonstrates how to use `SelectModel` to load options dynamically from a database/API within `TableManager`.

## SelectModel Overview

The `SelectModel` (defined in `lib/Runtime.Widget/bay/SelectModel.bay`) allows rendering select inputs.
- It stores options in `Vector<Map> options`.
- It supports dynamic data loading via the `loadData` method.
- It can define custom option structures using `options_type`.

## Dynamic Loading in TableManager

When configuring `TableManager`, you can define a form field as a `Runtime.Widget.Select` component. To populate it dynamically:

1.  Define the field in `form_fields`.
2.  Set the component to `Runtime.Widget.Select`.
3.  Implement `loadData` in your `PageModel` to fetch options and assign them to the specific `SelectModel` instance.

### Example: Dynamic Select in UserPageModel

In this example, we load roles from an API instead of hardcoding them.

```
namespace App;

use Runtime.ApiResult;
use Runtime.BaseModel;
use Runtime.RenderContainer;
use Runtime.Serializer.IntegerType;
use Runtime.Serializer.ObjectType;
use Runtime.Serializer.StringType;
use Runtime.Web.RouteInfo;
use Runtime.Widget.Table.TableManager;
use Runtime.Widget.SelectModel;

class UserPageModel extends BaseModel
{
	string component = classof UserPage;
	TableManager manager = null;
	SelectModel role_select = null;
	
	
	/**
	 * Serialize object
	 */
	static void serialize(ObjectType rules)
	{
		parent(rules);
		rules.addType("manager", new ObjectType());
	}
	
	
	/**
	 * Init params
	 */
	void initParams(Map params)
	{
		parent(params);
	}
	
	
	/**
	 * Init widget
	 */
	void initWidget(Map params)
	{
		parent(params);
		
		/* Initialize SelectModel for roles */
		this.role_select = this.createWidget(classof SelectModel, {
			"options_type": {
				"key": new StringType(),
				"value": new StringType(),
			}
		});
		
		/* Initialize TableManager */
		this.manager = this.createWidget(classof TableManager, {
			"autoload": true,
			"api_name": "app.user",
			"title": method this.getTitle,
			"item_rules": {
				"name": new StringType(),
				"email": new StringType(),
				"role": new StringType(),
			},
			"primary_rules": {
				"id": new IntegerType(),
			},
			"form_fields":
			[
				{
					"name": "name",
					"label": "Name",
					"component": "Runtime.Widget.Input",
				},
				{
					"name": "email",
					"label": "Email",
					"component": "Runtime.Widget.Input",
				},
				{
					"name": "role",
					"label": "Role",
					"component": "Runtime.Widget.Select",
					/* Pass the model instance to the component */
					"model": this.role_select 
				}
			],
			"table_fields":
			[
				{
					"name": "row_number",
				},
				{
					"name": "name",
					"label": "Name",
				},
				{
					"name": "email",
					"label": "Email",
				},
				{
					"name": "role",
					"label": "Role",
				},
				{
					"name": "buttons",
					"slot": "row_buttons"
				}
			]
		});
	}
	
	
	/**
	 * Load data
	 */
	async void loadData(RenderContainer container)
	{
		await parent(container);
		
		/* Load options for the role select dynamically */
		await this.loadRoleOptions();
	}
	
	
	/**
	 * Load role options from API
	 */
	async void loadRoleOptions()
	{
		ApiResult result = await this.layout.sendApi({
			"api_name": "app.role",
			"method_name": "list",
			"data": {}
		});
		
		if (result.isSuccess())
		{
			Vector<Map> roles = result.data.get("roles");
			Vector options = roles.map(Map (Map role) => {
				"key": role.get("id"),
				"value": role.get("name"),
			});
			
			/* Set options to the SelectModel instance */
			this.role_select.setOptions(roles);
		}
	}
	
	
	/**
	 * Build title
	 */
	void buildTitle(RenderContainer container)
	{
		this.layout.setPageTitle("Users page");
	}
	
	
	/**
	 * Returns title
	 */
	string getTitle(string action, Map title)
	{
		if (action == "add") return "Create user";
		else if (action == "edit") return "Edit user";
		else if (action == "delete") return "Delete user";
		else if (action == "delete_message") return "Delete";
		return "";
	}
}
```

**Key Points:**
- `role_select` is created as a child widget in `initWidget`.
- It is passed to the `TableManager` form field definition via `"model": this.role_select`.
- In `loadData`, we call `await this.loadRoleOptions()` which fetches data from an API.
- We update the select model using `this.role_select.setOptions(roles)`.

# Filter in TableManager

To add filter you should:

```
use Runtime.Widget.Table.FilterModel;

class UserPageModel extends BaseModel
{
	FilterModel filter = null;

	static void serialize(ObjectType rules)
	{
		parent(rules);
		rules.addType("filter", new ObjectType());
	}

	void initWidget()
	{
		this.filter = this.createWidget(classof FilterModel, {
			"fields":
			{
				"name": "name",
				"label": "Name",
				"component": "Runtime.Widget.Input",
			}
		});
	}
}
```

Add component in table wrap slot filter:
```
<TableWrap>
	<slot name="filter">
		%render this.renderWidget(this.model.filter);
	</slot>
</TableWrap>
```